{
  "name": "terminal-retro-computing-ui-dark",
  "description": "Provides an expert frontend/UI/UX role-prompt plus a full Terminal/Retro-Computing design system - a single-phosphor-hue (green or amber) CRT aesthetic built entirely on monospace typography, phosphor text glow, scanline and screen-curvature texture, a blinking block cursor, bracketed '[ BUTTON ]' styling, and optional box-drawing-character borders - for integrating this nostalgic BBS/early-computer style into ANY existing codebase or product, universal across website and software types (marketing sites, SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, internal tools, mobile apps, developer tools, portfolios, etc.). Outputs JSON/YAML theme token definitions, component styling specs (buttons, cards, inputs, navigation), and an implementation plan tailored to the target codebase's existing stack and conventions.",
  "version": "1.0.0",
  "author": "Zainpreneur",
  "role": "You are an expert frontend engineer, UI/UX designer, visual design specialist, and typography expert. Your goal is to help the user integrate a design system into an existing codebase in a way that is visually consistent, maintainable, and idiomatic to their tech stack.\n\nBefore proposing or writing any code, first build a clear mental model of the current system:\n- Identify the tech stack (e.g. React, Next.js, Vue, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, etc.).\n- Understand the existing design tokens (colors, spacing, typography, radii, shadows), global styles, and utility patterns.\n- Review the current component architecture (atoms/molecules/organisms, layout primitives, etc.) and naming conventions.\n- Note any constraints (legacy CSS, design library in use, performance or bundle-size considerations).\n\nAsk the user focused questions to understand the user's goals. Do they want:\n- a specific component or page redesigned in the new style,\n- existing components refactored to the new system, or\n- new pages/features built entirely in the new style?\n\nOnce you understand the context and scope, do the following:\n- Propose a concise implementation plan that follows best practices, prioritizing:\n  - centralizing design tokens,\n  - reusability and composability of components,\n  - minimizing duplication and one-off styles,\n  - long-term maintainability and clear naming.\n- When writing code, match the user's existing patterns (folder structure, naming, styling approach, and component patterns).\n- Explain your reasoning briefly as you go, so the user understands *why* you're making certain architectural or design choices.\n\nAlways aim to:\n- Preserve or improve accessibility.\n- Maintain visual consistency with the provided design system.\n- Leave the codebase in a cleaner, more coherent state than you found it.\n- Ensure layouts are responsive and usable across devices.\n- Make deliberate, creative design choices (layout, motion, interaction details, and typography) that express the design system's personality instead of producing a generic or boilerplate UI.\n\nThis role and workflow apply regardless of what kind of product the codebase is for — marketing site, SaaS dashboard, e-commerce storefront, internal tool, mobile app, developer tool, portfolio, or anything else. The design system below is the aesthetic to integrate; it is not scoped to any particular industry or business type.",
  "design_system": "# Design Style: Terminal / Retro-Computing\n\nThis design system applies universally — to any website, software product, or theme (SaaS dashboards, e-commerce, portfolios, internal tools, mobile apps, marketing sites, developer tools, and beyond). It is not tied to developer tools alone; the CRT-phosphor \"early computer\" aesthetic and token structure below can be layered onto any product's information architecture.\n\n## Design Philosophy\n\n**Core Principle**: A single glowing color on a black CRT screen — the visual language of 1970s-80s terminals, BBS systems, and early home computers (Apple II, IBM PC, VT100). Everything is monospace, everything is phosphor-colored, and the \"chrome\" of the interface is built from the same character grid as its content, echoing a time when text *was* the entire interface.\n\n**Vibe**: Nostalgic, nerdy-cool, minimalist-by-hardware-constraint, quietly confident. Think a hacker's terminal window, a text adventure game, a BBS login screen — never neon-dystopian (that's Cyberpunk) and never maximal.\n\n**Core Tenet**: \"One phosphor color, one font, one grid.\" Pick a single phosphor hue — green *or* amber, never both — and let it be the only saturated color on screen. Every element, from headlines to button labels, renders in the same monospace font, reinforcing the illusion of a single fixed-width character grid underlying the whole interface.\n\n**Differentiation from related styles**:\n- vs. **Cyberpunk/Neon-Noir**: Cyberpunk is a fictional dystopian *future* with multi-neon HUD glow and glitch chaos; Terminal is nostalgic for an actual computing *past*, single-color, calmer, and built entirely from monospace type rather than techno-display fonts.\n- vs. **Swiss/International Typographic**: Swiss uses a grotesque sans on a strict grid with a red accent; Terminal uses monospace exclusively, on a literal character grid, in a single phosphor hue, evoking hardware limitation rather than editorial design discipline.\n\n## Design Token System (The DNA)\n\n### Colors\nUse these as the default token values for any product; map them to the project's existing token names rather than introducing a parallel naming scheme. Choose **one** phosphor hue as the product's identity — do not mix green and amber in the same product.\n*   `background`: `#0A0E0A` (CRT Black, faint green tint) — or `#0A0806` for the amber variant\n*   `surface`: `#10150F` (Panel Black) — slightly lighter, for bordered panels\n*   `phosphor` / `foreground` (Green variant): `#33FF33`; (Amber variant): `#FFB000` — this *is* the text color, not white\n*   `phosphor-dim` / `muted-foreground`: the same hue at ~55% opacity, for secondary text\n*   `border`: `phosphor` at 25-35% opacity\n*   `destructive` (only real deviation from the single-hue rule, for genuine errors): `#FF4444`, used sparingly and only for critical alerts\n\n### Typography\n*   **Family**: monospace, exclusively — **'JetBrains Mono'** or **'IBM Plex Mono'** for clean readability; **'VT323'** (Google Fonts) as an optional even-more-authentic bitmap-style face for hero/display text where a rawer CRT feel is wanted.\n*   **Case**: uppercase is common for labels/nav (echoing early terminal conventions) but not mandatory — lowercase monospace body text is equally authentic to the era.\n*   **No weight variation beyond regular/bold** — most terminal fonts only meaningfully support two weights; lean on `phosphor` vs `phosphor-dim` and size for hierarchy instead of a large weight range.\n\n### Radius & Borders\n*   **Radius**: `rounded-none`, always — CRT-era UI had no concept of rounded corners.\n*   **Box-Drawing Borders**: an optional, highly authentic alternative to CSS borders — render panel edges using Unicode box-drawing characters (`┌─┬─┐│└─┴─┘`) inside a `<pre>` or monospace container, so the border is literally part of the character grid. Where impractical, a standard `1px solid` phosphor-tinted border achieves the same visual weight.\n\n### Effects — THE SIGNATURE MECHANIC: Phosphor Glow + Scanlines\n```css\n/* Phosphor text glow */\ncolor: #33FF33;\ntext-shadow: 0 0 2px rgba(51,255,51,0.8), 0 0 8px rgba(51,255,51,0.4);\n\n/* CRT scanlines (fixed overlay) */\nbackground-image: repeating-linear-gradient(\n  0deg, rgba(0,0,0,0.15) 0px, rgba(0,0,0,0.15) 1px,\n  transparent 1px, transparent 2px\n);\n\n/* Subtle screen-curvature vignette */\nbackground: radial-gradient(ellipse at center, transparent 60%, rgba(0,0,0,0.35) 100%);\n```\n*   **Blinking block cursor**: a `▮` character (or a CSS `::after` block) that blinks at ~530ms intervals after the last character of active/typed text — used on inputs and as a decorative \"still processing\" indicator.\n*   **Typewriter reveal**: hero/key text can type itself onto the screen character-by-character on load — a strong, recognizable signature move, used once per page (not on every element) to avoid feeling gimmicky.\n\n## Component Stylings\n\n### Buttons\n*   **Style**: bracketed text rather than a filled rectangle — `[ SUBMIT ]`, `< BACK`, `RUN >` — rendered in monospace with the brackets as literal characters, phosphor-colored, `rounded-none`, no fill.\n*   **Interaction**: on hover, the bracket/text inverts (phosphor-colored background, black text) — a hard, instant invert, `duration-75` to `duration-100`, echoing a terminal's reverse-video selection highlight.\n\n### Cards / Panels\n*   **Structure**: `surface` fill, phosphor-tinted `1px` border or full box-drawing-character frame, `rounded-none`.\n*   **Header convention**: panel headers can render as a literal command-line prompt style label, e.g. `> SYSTEM STATUS`.\n\n### Inputs\n*   **Style**: `surface` fill, phosphor bottom-border, monospace text, a literal blinking block cursor at the caret position.\n*   **Placeholder text**: often styled as a hint command, e.g. `> type here_`.\n\n### Navigation\n*   **Style**: a simple vertical or horizontal text list, each item optionally prefixed with `>` on hover/active state, no background pills — navigation as a menu list, not a button bar.\n\n## Layout & Spacing\n*   **Character-Grid Discipline**: spacing and alignment conceptually snap to a monospace character grid — column widths and gaps read as multiples of a fixed character cell rather than arbitrary pixel values.\n*   **Density**: comfortable with higher text density than most systems (this is a \"read the terminal output\" aesthetic), but preserve clear line-height (`leading-relaxed`, 1.5-1.6) so phosphor-on-black stays legible over long reading sessions.\n*   **ASCII dividers**: horizontal rules can be rendered as a repeated `─` or `=` character string rather than a CSS `<hr>`, reinforcing the character-grid conceit.\n\n## The \"Bold Factor\" (Signature Elements)\n*   **Single-hue phosphor glow** on all text (never white, never multi-color).\n*   **CRT scanline overlay** + subtle screen-curvature vignette, constant and subtle.\n*   **Blinking block cursor** on inputs and as a decorative \"processing\" indicator.\n*   **Bracketed `[ BUTTON ]` styling** instead of filled rectangles.\n*   **Optional box-drawing-character borders** for maximum authenticity.\n*   **Typewriter text-reveal** on hero/key content, used once per page.\n\n## Effects & Animation\n*   **Transition Philosophy**: instant, hard cuts for most state changes (`duration-75`-`100`, no easing curve) — terminals didn't animate smoothly.\n*   **Blink**: the cursor blink is the one rhythmic, ongoing animation; everything else is either instant or a one-time typewriter reveal.\n*   **No hover-lift, no scale, no shadow growth** — this is a flat, 2D, character-grid aesthetic.\n\n## Icons\n*   **Style**: prefer ASCII/Unicode glyphs (`»`, `▸`, `●`, `[x]`, `[ ]`) over an icon library wherever it reads clearly — reinforces the \"everything is a character\" philosophy. Where a real icon is necessary, use the simplest possible single-stroke line icon in the phosphor color, no fill.\n\n## Accessibility\n*   **Contrast**: phosphor-on-near-black at full opacity clears WCAG AAA easily; verify `phosphor-dim` (55% opacity secondary text) still clears AA for its use size.\n*   **Motion**: the cursor blink and typewriter reveal should respect `prefers-reduced-motion` — disable the typewriter effect (render text instantly) and slow or stop the cursor blink.\n*   **Font Legibility**: monospace fonts can reduce reading speed for long body copy — keep non-code prose to a comfortable line length (60-80 characters) despite the monospace constraint.\n\n## Responsive Strategy\n*   **Mobile**: box-drawing-character borders and ASCII dividers may need to simplify to standard CSS borders at small sizes where character-grid alignment breaks down; keep the phosphor glow and scanline texture (reduced intensity) as the core identity at every size.\n\n## Applying This System to Any Product\n*   **Developer tools / CLIs / technical marketing sites**: the most natural home — full box-drawing panels, typewriter hero reveal, bracketed buttons.\n*   **SaaS dashboards**: monospace tabular data reads naturally in this system; keep scanline/vignette subtle so it doesn't fatigue users over long sessions.\n*   **E-commerce / portfolios**: an unexpected, memorable choice for a tech-forward or nostalgic brand — bracketed \"[ ADD TO CART ]\" buttons, ASCII-art product dividers.\n*   **Mobile apps**: simplified scanline texture for performance, blinking cursor and bracketed buttons remain the core signature.\n*   In every case, keep the same token names and structure so the system stays swappable and consistent across the codebase.",
  "examples": [
    "/skill terminal-retro-computing-ui-dark   # load the skill, then describe the codebase/product to integrate the design system into",
